<TextPage
  title="About HearMyMic: A Free, Private Mic Test"
  description="HearMyMic is a free online microphone and webcam test that runs entirely in your browser. Learn how it works and what it can and can’t measure."
  path="/about"
  h1="About HearMyMic"
  crumb="About"
>
  <p>
    <span translate="no">HearMyMic</span> is a free tool for one job: checking that your microphone works and hearing how
    you sound, before a call, a stream or a recording.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <p>
    When you press “Test my mic”, your browser asks for permission to use your microphone. Once you allow it, the page
    reads the audio signal directly on your device and draws the level meter and waveform you see on screen. Recording
    and playback, live monitoring and the camera preview all work the same way: inside your browser tab.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no server receiving your audio or video, no account, and no app to install. When you close the page, the
    microphone turns off and any recording is gone.
  </p>

  <h2>What it can and can’t tell you</h2>
  <p>
    The test is a practical check. It tells you whether a microphone is connected and allowed, which device your browser
    is using, how strong your signal is, and how you sound on a short recording.
  </p>
  <p>
    It isn’t a calibrated measuring instrument. Levels, settings and latency are based on what your browser reports, and
    browsers process audio before a web page sees it. Apps such as Zoom, Teams and Discord then add processing of their
    own. Treat the results as a good guide, not as exact figures.
  </p>

  <h2>Cost and privacy</h2>
  <p>
    <span translate="no">HearMyMic</span> is free to use, with no account and no sign-up. See the
    <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a> for how your data is handled.
  </p>

  <h2>Get in touch</h2>
  <p>
    Found a problem, or a device the test doesn’t handle well? We’d like to hear about it. Details are on the
    <a href="/contact">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</TextPage>
